# Plan Creation Contract

**Activation:** `alwaysApply: false` — when triggers in `agent-discipline-core.mdc` match, **Read this file before editing**. Triggered rules are mandatory, not optional.

Applies to **every plan**: pre-code chat plans, **`HPCPerfStats/monitor/docs/plans/`** design docs, monitor **Consumer follow-up plan** sections, and Cursor Plan mode output.

**Authoring entrypoint:** read and match **`HPCPerfStats/monitor/docs/plans/PLAN_TEMPLATE.md`**. **`plan-template-enforcement.mdc`** and **`plan-completion-gate.mdc`** restate the non-skippable **close sequence** (senior final code review + structured chat self-review) for **all non-trivial code changes**; plan YAML + **`post-implementation-review`** todo when implementing a plan.

## Grounding (no guessing)

- Base every claim on **verified facts** from the repo (code, tests, logs, committed docs) — not assumptions.
- Read callers, tests, and applicable workspace rules before stating current behavior or scope.
- For consumer contract questions, read **`HPCPerfStats/hpcperfstats/listend.py`** (read-only) per **`monitor-workspace-contract.mdc`**.
- If a requirement, API, config key, acceptance criterion, or best-practice choice is unclear: **ask the user**; **do not guess** or invent context.

## Industry best practices

- Anchor the approach in recognized standards for the relevant expertise (e.g. Autotools/C portability, HPC daemon design, RabbitMQ ingest contracts).
- **Call out** which guidelines inform the plan (name the practice or source briefly, e.g. test pyramid — unit `make check` before full static bundle).
- Prefer project rules (`monitor-c-testing-standards.mdc`, `monitor-static-build-verification.mdc`) where they already encode local best practice.

## Required plan steps (in order)

1. **Problem and facts** — constraints, verified current behavior, affected layers.
2. **Approach** — ordered steps with trade-offs tied to the best-practice callouts above.
3. **Testing** — every behavior change must include at least one **regression test and/or unit test** at the narrowest layer that proves the contract. Cite existing `tests/test_*.c` modules or name new ones. For fixes, follow **`every-error-regression-test.mdc`** and **`test-first-discipline.mdc`**.
4. **Implementation** — what will change (files/layers). Omit for answer-only or doc-only work with no logic change.
5. **Cursor rules** — enshrine modalities, best practices, contracts, and failure modes that should guide future work:
 - When this plan introduces or clarifies a **recurring** pattern, **add or update** a focused **`.mdc`** rule in **`HPCPerfStats/monitor/cursor-rules/`** (authoritative; symlinked from **`.cursor/rules/`**).
 - **Every new or re-scoped domain rule** must be registered in the **task router** in **`agent-discipline-core.mdc`** in the **same task** and in **`cursor-hooks/hook_task_router.py`** (`MONITOR_ROUTER_ENTRIES`). Default **`alwaysApply: false`**; add **`globs:`** when possible.
 - **Prefer updating** an existing rule over creating a duplicate; cross-link related rules in an **Overlap** section.
 - Keep rules narrow and actionable; base content on **verified facts** from this plan, not assumptions.
 - If nothing merits a new or updated rule, state **no rule change needed** with one sentence of rationale.
 - If rule scope or wording is unclear, **ask the user** before writing the **`.mdc`** text.
6. **Final code review (mandatory last step)** — at implementation close, act as a **Senior Software Engineer**. Review the **full diff** and every **workflow the change touches** (callers, tests, static bundle + cross-compile, consumer contract, docs sync—not only files edited). Look for:
   - correctness and behavior regressions
   - performance regressions
   - missing components (tests, docs, contract tests, spec/version sync)
   - edge cases not covered by tests
   - **new or re-triggered domain rules** wired into **both** `agent-discipline-core.mdc` (task router) and `cursor-hooks/hook_task_router.py` (`MONITOR_ROUTER_ENTRIES`) in the same task
   - anything else a senior reviewer would block on merge  
   **Fix anything found** and re-run relevant tests. Do not deliver structured chat self-review or mark todos complete while open review items remain. Execution detail: **`plan-completion-gate.mdc`** close-sequence step 1.

## Plan YAML todos (required shape)

Include a **final** todo the agent cannot skip:

```yaml
- id: post-implementation-review
  content: "Final senior code review (diff + workflow) clean; structured self-review in chat (why it works, edge cases, convention check)"
  status: pending
```

Copy the full todo block from **`HPCPerfStats/monitor/docs/plans/PLAN_TEMPLATE.md`** when authoring new plans.

## Post-implementation review (completion bar section)

Every implementation plan must end with this section (checkboxes for the implementing agent):

```markdown
## Post-implementation review (required before close)

Per `plan-completion-gate.mdc` close-sequence step 2 and `implementation-review-workflow.mdc` (after senior final code review is clean):

- [ ] **Why it works** — one paragraph, contracts cited
- [ ] **Edge cases** — ≥3 realistic failure modes named
- [ ] **Convention check** — tests, triggered rules, layer wiring; senior review pass had no unfixed gaps
- [ ] Regression tests for any fixed/discovered errors (`every-error-regression-test.mdc`)
- [ ] Test run logged under `test_runs/` when tests executed (`test-runs-output-directory.mdc`)
- [ ] Runtime logic: `logic-change-checklist.mdc` satisfied (if applicable)
```

The implementing agent writes the three narrative sections (**Why it works**, **Edge cases**, **Convention check**) in chat when closing the task—not only checking boxes in the plan file. If chat self-review surfaces a new gap, return to final code review, fix, re-test, then update the chat sections.

## Completion bar

A plan — and the work it describes — is **not complete** until:

- tests are added or extended and executed (or blockers documented per **`test-first-discipline.mdc`**),
- the Cursor rules step is done (rule added/updated, or explicit no-rule rationale),
- plan YAML todos are synced (`status: completed` per **`plan-completion-gate.mdc`** → *Plan YAML todo sync*),
- **final code review** (senior-engineer pass on diff and affected workflows) is done with no unfixed findings,
- **`post-implementation-review`** todo is completed only after structured chat self-review is delivered,
- conclusions trace to verified facts.

## Narrow exceptions

- Answer-only questions with no implementation.
- Trivial typo or comment-only edits with no behavioral contract change.

## Overlap

- **`plan-template-enforcement.mdc`** — enforcement reminder and user-request phrasing (“per PLAN_TEMPLATE.md”, “include final code review”).
- **`plan-completion-gate.mdc`** — agent execution gate: senior final code review then structured chat self-review before close.
- **`implementation-review-workflow.mdc`** — pre-code breakdown and post-code self-review; this rule adds mandatory plan structure, test requirement, and review-as-final-step.
- **`logic-change-checklist.mdc`** — use during the final review step for runtime logic.
- **`test-first-discipline.mdc`**, **`every-error-regression-test.mdc`** — test layer selection and mandatory regression coverage.
- **`monitor-consumer-side-plan.mdc`** — consumer handoff plans must include testing and final review sections.
- **`test-runs-output-directory.mdc`** — committed **`docs/plans/`** hold design baselines; run artifacts stay under **`test_runs/`**.
- **`chat-analysis-for-rules.mdc`** — on-demand transcript mining for rule ideas; this rule requires enshrining patterns discovered during implementation plans in the same task when clear.
